Due to the tendency for high-energy lepton colliders to radiate forward, weak interactions at the peak energy of these machines require a sophisticated theoretical framework. This paper introduces a new approach called the effective vector boson approximation (EVA) specifically for muon colliders, building upon the established effective parton luminosity approximation (ELA) for electron-positron colliders. Within this EVA framework, hadronic interactions at high energies can be analyzed using well-established particle physics models like QCD, electroweak theories, and vector meson dominance. Key applications of this EVA approach include the calculation of heavy vector lepton and quark pair production, as well as $W$ and $Z$ boson fusion processes. Furthermore, we present EVA predictions for specific processes at the muon collider, such as $\mu^+\mu^-	o 	au^+	au^-$, $\mu^+\mu^-	o 	au^+ 
